K20-K21  TASK 4 -- the identification: u = 1+3<w> presupposes the REST mass in the denominator
====================================================================================================
  u = P_law/(D-B): numerator = the law's phantom, denominator = the observed excess over
  baryons.  The excess is an ACTIVE mass (it is what lenses).  So u = 1+3w only if the
  denominator is the cold REST mass, i.e. if
      M_rest(inside) = M_active/(1+3w) = [the u=1 reading] x 1/(1+3w) .
  A18's amplitude inversion fixes the ACTIVE ratio: R_req = 5.552 +- 0.427 (0.44 sigma from
  R_cosmic = 5.364).  With the cold rest mass = f_ret R_cosmic M_b and f_ret <= 1:
      (1+3w) f_ret R_cosmic = R_req   =>   f_ret = (R_req/R_cosmic)/(1+3w) .
      INNER : 1/(1+3w) = 2.987   f_ret = 3.091 +- 0.520   (4.0 sigma above 1)
      MID   : 1/(1+3w) = 2.121   f_ret = 2.195 +- 0.336   (3.6 sigma above 1)
      OUTER : 1/(1+3w) = 1.014   f_ret = 1.049 +- 0.105   (0.5 sigma above 1)
K20 PASS f_ret(MID) = 2.195 +- 0.336 (3.6 sigma above 1); f_ret(INNER) = 3.091 +- 0.520 > 2.0 / > 2.5, > 3 sigma :: the EOS reading needs 2.12x (MID) / 2.99x (INNER) the cold rest mass that the u=1 reading assigns, i.e. f_ret = 2.20 +- 0.34 (MID) and 3.09 +- 0.52 (INNER), against the ceiling f_ret = 1 imposed by P4 (cold rest mass = the catchment's cosmic share). The SAME amplitudes that give u = 0.47 exclude the EOS interpretation at 3.6 sigma (MID) / 4.0 sigma (INNER) once the normalization is imposed. The MID error is correlated (f_ret = lambda^2 R_tot/R_cosmic), so 3.6 sigma, not the 5.3 sigma an independent-error treatment would give
K21 PASS shortfalls vs 0.176: kinetic 2.16e+08, gravitational 2.35e+04, field 5.58e+03, potential depth 4.45e+04 all > 1e3 :: routes (i), (ii) and (iii) fail for one reason, not three: every stress the cold sector can carry is bounded by rho x max(sigma^2, |Phi|, a0^2/(8 pi G rho)) = 0.18 rho c^2 x 10^-4ish, and the measurement needs 0.18 rho c^2. The obstruction is dimensional: the cold sector is non-relativistic (THEORY s3.3), and w ~ -1/6 is a rest-mass-scale statement

